        {
          "subsection_id": "tuning_fork_subsection_2",
          "subsection_title": "1. Identification and definition",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "tuning_fork_group_2",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A tuning fork is a metal instrument with two prongs that vibrate at a specific frequency when struck. It is used in clinical examination to assess hearing and the sensation of vibration."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "In an OSCE, you should be able to identify the instrument, state its frequency, demonstrate the relevant test, interpret the findings and explain its limitations."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "tuning_fork_subsection_3",
          "subsection_title": "2. Types and frequencies",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "tuning_fork_group_3",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/tuning_fork_02.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "128 Hz tuning fork"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Vibration sense"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Commonly used to assess vibration sensation during the neurological examination, particularly in the feet and toes."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/tuning_fork_03.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "512 Hz tuning fork"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Hearing tests"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Commonly preferred for the Rinne and Weber tests because it produces a useful tone with relatively little tactile vibration."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/tuning_fork_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "256 Hz tuning fork May be encountered in older or alternative examination protocols; 512 Hz is generally preferred for bedside hearing tests."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Exam tip: Always state the frequency you are using and the purpose of the test. Do not confuse the 128 Hz fork commonly used for vibration sense with the 512 Hz fork commonly used for Rinne and Weber."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "tuning_fork_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "3. Parts of the instrument",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "tuning_fork_group_4",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/tuning_fork_05.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "gpraj.com"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Prongs: The two arms that vibrate and produce the tone.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Stem: The central portion used to hold the fork.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Base or footplate: The end placed against the mastoid or a bony prominence.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "tuning_fork_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "4. Clinical uses",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "tuning_fork_group_5",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Rinne test: Compares air conduction with bone conduction.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Weber test: Assesses whether sound lateralizes to one ear.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Vibration sense: Assesses large-fibre peripheral sensory pathways.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Selected historical bedside tests: Tuning forks have been used to investigate suspected fractures, but these tests are unreliable and should not replace appropriate imaging.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "tuning_fork_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "5. Advantages and disadvantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "tuning_fork_group_6",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Advantages",
                    "Disadvantages"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Portable, inexpensive and reusable",
                      "Subjective; depends on patient cooperation"
                    ],
                    [
                      "No electricity required",
                      "Cannot quantify hearing loss"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Useful for bedside screening",
                      "Results can be affected by technique and background noise"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Helps distinguish patterns of conductive and sensorineural hearing loss",
                      "Does not replace formal audiometry"
                    ]
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "tuning_fork_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "6. Preparation and precautions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "tuning_fork_group_7",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "Before performing a tuning fork test:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Introduce yourself, confirm the patient's identity and explain the procedure.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Obtain consent and ensure the patient can understand the instructions.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Perform hearing tests in a reasonably quiet environment.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Check whether the patient uses hearing aids and remove them if appropriate for the test.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Strike the fork gently against the heel of your hand or a soft rubber surface.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Avoid striking it against metal or another hard object.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Do not touch the vibrating prongs while testing.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Avoid placing the fork on an injured or painful area.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "tuning_fork_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "7. Rinne test — air conduction versus bone conduction",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "tuning_fork_group_8",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/tuning_fork_06.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/tuning_fork_07.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/tuning_fork_08.webp",
                  "caption": null
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "tuning_fork_group_9",
              "group_title": "Principle",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "The Rinne test compares:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Air conduction (AC): Sound travels through the external auditory canal, tympanic membrane and middle ear to the cochlea.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Bone conduction (BC): Vibration travels through the skull to stimulate the cochlea, bypassing the external and middle ear.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "In normal hearing, air conduction is more effective than bone conduction."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "tuning_fork_group_10",
              "group_title": "Step-by-step procedure",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Explain that the patient will hear a sound and should tell you when it is no longer audible.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Activate a 512 Hz tuning fork gently.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Place the base firmly on the patient's mastoid process, behind the ear. This tests bone conduction.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Ask the patient to indicate when the sound disappears.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Immediately move the still-vibrating fork beside the external auditory canal without touching the ear. This tests air conduction.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Ask whether the sound can be heard again. Repeat on the opposite ear.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "tuning_fork_group_11",
              "group_title": "Interpretation",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Result",
                    "Meaning"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "AC greater than BC — Rinne positive",
                      "Normal hearing or sensorineural hearing loss"
                    ],
                    [
                      "BC equal to or greater than AC — Rinne negative",
                      "Suggests conductive hearing loss in that ear"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Important: A positive Rinne test does not necessarily mean hearing is normal. It can also occur in sensorineural hearing loss."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "tuning_fork_group_12",
              "group_title": "Examples of abnormal findings",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Right conductive hearing loss: Rinne may be negative on the right, with BC equal to or greater than AC.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Left sensorineural hearing loss: Rinne is often positive on both sides, despite reduced hearing on the left.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Severe unilateral sensorineural hearing loss: Rinne can be falsely negative if the opposite cochlea detects sound transmitted through the skull.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The last point is an important OSCE limitation: if the result does not fit the clinical picture, further assessment and formal audiometry are required."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "tuning_fork_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "8. Weber test — sound lateralization",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "tuning_fork_group_13",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/tuning_fork_09.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/tuning_fork_10.webp",
                  "caption": null
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "tuning_fork_group_14",
              "group_title": "Principle",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The Weber test assesses whether a sound conducted through the skull is perceived equally in both ears or louder in one ear."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "tuning_fork_group_15",
              "group_title": "Step-by-step procedure",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Activate a 512 Hz tuning fork.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Place the base on the midline of the forehead or vertex.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Ask the patient: “Do you hear the sound equally in both ears, or is it louder on one side?”",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Record whether the sound is central, equal or lateralized to the right or left.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "tuning_fork_group_16",
              "group_title": "Interpretation",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Finding",
                    "Likely interpretation"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Sound heard centrally or equally",
                      "Normal hearing or symmetrical hearing loss"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Sound louder in the affected ear",
                      "Suggests conductive hearing loss in that ear"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Sound louder in the better ear",
                      "Suggests sensorineural hearing loss in the opposite ear"
                    ]
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "tuning_fork_group_17",
              "group_title": "Memory tip",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "“Conductive goes to the bad ear; sensorineural goes to the good ear.”",
                  "style": "quote"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "This is a useful rule for typical unilateral hearing loss. Interpretation must still account for the patient's history, degree of hearing loss and possibility of mixed or bilateral disease."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "tuning_fork_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "9. Combined Rinne and Weber interpretation",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "tuning_fork_group_18",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Clinical situation",
                    "Rinne",
                    "Weber"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Normal hearing",
                      "Positive in both ears",
                      "Midline/equal"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Right conductive hearing loss",
                      "Negative on right",
                      "Lateralizes right"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Left conductive hearing loss",
                      "Negative on left",
                      "Lateralizes left"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Right sensorineural hearing loss",
                      "Usually positive bilaterally",
                      "Lateralizes left"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Left sensorineural hearing loss",
                      "Usually positive bilaterally",
                      "Lateralizes right"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "These are typical patterns, not absolute rules. Bedside tests are screening tools; formal audiometry is needed to characterize hearing loss accurately."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "tuning_fork_subsection_11",
          "subsection_title": "10. Testing vibration sense",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "tuning_fork_group_19",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/instrumentation/tuning_fork_11.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "clinician.com"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "tuning_fork_group_20",
              "group_title": "Indications",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "Vibration sense is part of the sensory examination, especially when assessing:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Peripheral neuropathy, including diabetic neuropathy.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Posterior column dysfunction.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Suspected loss of distal large-fibre sensation.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "tuning_fork_group_21",
              "group_title": "Procedure",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Explain the test and demonstrate the sensation on the patient's hand.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Ask the patient to close their eyes.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Activate a 128 Hz tuning fork.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Place the base on a bony prominence, such as the distal joint of the great toe.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Ask the patient to report when vibration begins and when it stops.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Compare both sides. If sensation is reduced, move proximally to determine the extent of the deficit.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Precautions: Avoid soft tissue, compare equivalent sites and ensure the patient is not merely detecting pressure from the fork."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "tuning_fork_group_22",
              "group_title": "Reporting findings",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Normal: “Vibration sense is intact and symmetrical at the distal joints of both great toes.”",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Abnormal: “Vibration sense is reduced bilaterally at the great toes and is better preserved at the ankles.”",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Interpret reduced vibration in the context of the complete neurological examination."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "tuning_fork_subsection_12",
          "subsection_title": "11. Advantages, limitations and common errors",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "tuning_fork_group_23",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "Common errors that lose OSCE marks:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Using the wrong frequency without explaining why.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Striking the fork too forcefully or against a hard surface.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Performing Rinne in the wrong sequence: the mastoid is tested first, followed by air conduction beside the ear canal.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Calling a positive Rinne “normal hearing” without qualification.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Forgetting to test both ears.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Misinterpreting Weber lateralization.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Testing vibration over soft tissue rather than bone.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Treating tuning fork results as a definitive diagnosis without further assessment.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "tuning_fork_subsection_13",
          "subsection_title": "12. Pediatric considerations",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "tuning_fork_group_24",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Explain the procedure in simple, age-appropriate language.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Demonstrate the sound before starting.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Keep instructions short and check that the child understands.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Do not interpret an inconsistent response from a young or distressed child as definite hearing loss.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "If hearing impairment is suspected, arrange appropriate age-specific hearing assessment. Tuning fork testing does not replace newborn hearing screening.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "tuning_fork_subsection_14",
          "subsection_title": "13. Common examiner questions and model answers",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "tuning_fork_group_25",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. Which tuning fork is commonly used for Rinne and Weber tests?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A 512 Hz tuning fork."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. What does a positive Rinne test mean?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Air conduction is greater than bone conduction. This occurs in normal hearing and commonly in sensorineural hearing loss."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. What does a negative Rinne test suggest?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Bone conduction is equal to or greater than air conduction, suggesting conductive hearing loss."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. In unilateral conductive hearing loss, where does Weber lateralize?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "To the affected ear."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. In unilateral sensorineural hearing loss, where does Weber lateralize?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "To the better-hearing ear."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. Which tuning fork is commonly used for vibration sense?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "A 128 Hz tuning fork."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. What is the limitation of these tests?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "They are subjective bedside screening tests and cannot quantify hearing thresholds or replace formal audiometry."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "tuning_fork_subsection_15",
          "subsection_title": "14. Mini OSCE station",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "tuning_fork_group_26",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Station: A patient reports reduced hearing in the right ear. Perform and interpret Rinne and Weber tests."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "A good candidate should:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Introduce themselves, explain the tests and obtain consent.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Select a 512 Hz tuning fork.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Perform Rinne on both ears correctly.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Perform Weber at the midline.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "State the findings clearly.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Interpret the results in the context of conductive versus sensorineural hearing loss.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Explain that further hearing assessment may be needed.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "7."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Example report: “The Rinne test is negative in the right ear and positive in the left. Weber lateralizes to the right. These findings are consistent with right-sided conductive hearing loss. I would correlate with otoscopy and arrange formal audiological assessment as appropriate.”"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}